def w(name,purpose,steps,adapt,detail,exercise,limits):
 def tests(l):return detail+' Evidence comes from the actual '+l(CPP/'support/ObjectOracle.java','Java oracle')+', '+l(CPP/'support/objects.cpp','native harness')+' and '+l(CPP/'tools/object_differential.py','object differential driver')+'. The inspected full-object result is 9,244 matches with zero failures; it is not a per-file assertion count. Generic errors do not establish exact exception-class parity.'
 author('src/org/minima/objects/keys/'+name+'.md',purpose,steps,adapt,tests,exercise,limits+' Source-map status remains partial and parity not_verified.')
w('Signature','An ordered chain of SignatureProof objects linking a tree’s root public key down to a signature of the final message.',[
 'Start with an empty mutable list. addSignatureProof appends without deduplication or cryptographic checks.',
 'getRootPublicKey reads the first proof’s derived root. An empty Signature is serializable and has valid empty JSON, but asking it for a root fails on index zero.',
 'JSON contains a signatures array. Binary output writes a MiniNumber count followed by each proof in order.',
 'Java reading replaces the existing list before reading the count, then appends complete proofs. The convenience MiniData converter catches IOException, logs and returns null.',
 'Verification of the chain belongs to TreeKey.verify. Merely being able to decode this container does not authenticate its contents.'
], 'Native storage is a vector with mutable access. Its root getter explicitly throws a Java-typed IndexOutOfBoundsException for an empty list. Static deserialization returns a fresh object; the optional-returning converter catches all std::exception, broader than Java’s IOException catch.',
 'The signature branch creates 0, 1, 2 and 5 proof entries and compares bytes/JSON. It intentionally uses arbitrary short signature bytes, so this is container testing. Tree-key-derived verification cases separately check valid and altered signature chains.',
 'Why can an empty Signature round-trip successfully but fail when added to Witness? Trace the root-key lookup used by Witness.addSignature.',
 'Mutable list identity across reads, exact converter logging and exception breadth remain different. The empty-list getter’s typed fix does not establish every Signature error path.')
w('TreeKeyNode','One level of a deterministic signing-key tree: a collection of one-time keys committed by an MMR, plus lazily built child trees.',[
 'Remember the branching size and derive childSeed by hashing the serialized private seed. The child seed separates descendant derivation from this level’s individual key derivation.',
 'For each index i, hash serialized MiniNumber(i) and the original private seed together. Use that derived seed to construct a Winternitz key, then add its public key as a zero-sum MMR leaf.',
 'The MMR root becomes this node’s public key. Keys remain indexed so callers can retrieve the chosen one-time key and its membership proof.',
 'getChild checks the indexed cache. If absent, hash the index with childSeed and build a new node of the same size. Later calls reuse that child.',
 'A child also caches its parent-to-child SignatureProof. TreeKey can reuse this fixed signature of the child root while selecting different message keys below it.'
], 'The native node uses unique_ptr children, a vector of Winternitz values, a mapped MMR and an optional cached parent signature. It creates descendants only when requested. Negative/zero sizes and out-of-range indices use native exception categories that do not fully match Java array/null errors.',
 'The treekey fixture exercises this node indirectly through complete key construction, public-key equality, two successive signatures and verification, across branching sizes 1, 2 and 3 and several depths/use counts. This checks deterministic roots and selected descendant paths, but does not independently assert all cache object identities.',
 'Why use hash(index, childSeed) for child trees instead of hash(index, originalSeed), which already derives the keys at the current level?',
 'No direct proof of all child-cache alias behavior follows from matching signature bytes. A full lifetime review must also account for references returned to children and one-time keys.')
w('TreeKey','A stateful hierarchy of one-time signing keys. It selects a path from a usage counter and produces a chain of signatures ending at the message.',[
 'Store levels and keys per level, rejecting only levels above eight at this initial check. Compute maxUses by Math.pow followed by Java int conversion, initialize usage to zero and build the root TreeKeyNode.',
 'Before signing, if uses is at least maxUses, log a serious error and reset uses to zero. This is the pinned source’s observable exhaustion behavior; the class itself does not refuse key reuse.',
 'Convert uses into base keysPerLevel digits, pad to the requested levels and reverse to get the root-to-leaf path.',
 'At each selected key, obtain its public key and MMR proof. At intermediate levels, sign the child root once and cache that parent-child proof. At the final level, sign the actual message.',
 'Append proofs in root-to-leaf order and increment uses only after the loop succeeds. Errors before completion can leave lazily created children or cached signatures while leaving uses unchanged.',
 'Verification rejects more than eight proofs and rejects a first derived root different from the configured public key. Each intermediate signature must verify the next proof’s derived root; the final signature must verify the supplied message. An empty proof chain returns false.',
 'setPublicKey permits verification with a default-constructed public-only object. setUses directly replaces the counter without range validation; callers are responsible for persistent usage policy.'
], 'C++ preserves Java double-to-int saturation when computing maxUses and explicit integer wraparound when incrementing uses. It stores optional private/public values and unique ownership of the root, while an injectable logger supports testing. The nullable signing overload can reproduce a null message error at the final node.',
 'The driver uses a fixed seed, branch/depth/use combinations including the last available uses, zero depth, negative usage, zero keys and excessive depth. Successful cases produce two signatures and compare public key, maxUses, resulting usage counts, complete signature bytes and verification. Derived tests alter the message, public key and signature length, and separately test an empty chain.',
 'For two keys per level and three levels, write the path for uses=3. Which parent-child signatures can be reused when signing the next message at uses=4?',
 'The exhaustion reset must be documented faithfully but is not a safe application key-use policy. Wallet persistence and backup/restore must prevent unintended one-time key reuse. These fixtures establish selected parity behavior, not a cryptographic audit or complete durability guarantee.')
w('Winternitz','The Minima wrapper around Winternitz one-time signatures using SHA3-256 and the Bouncy Castle GMSS parameter w=8.',[
 'The Java seeded constructor passes private seed bytes, SHA3Digest(256) and parameter eight to WinternitzOTSignature, then caches its public key. Its default constructor leaves the signing engine and public key null.',
 'Java sign delegates message bytes to the library and returns signature bytes as MiniData. Java verify asks WinternitzOTSVerify to reconstruct a public key and compares it with the supplied key.',
 'The inspected native algorithm hashes the message to 32 byte-valued digits and computes two checksum digits from 8192 minus their sum. These give 34 hash-chain positions.',
 'Native private elements are generated from the first 32 seed bytes. Each iteration hashes the current seed, then updates the seed as a little-endian addition of seed + hash + 1 modulo 2^256.',
 'To derive the native public key, advance every private element through 255 SHA3 rounds, concatenate endpoints and hash them. Signing advances each chain only by its message/checksum digit.',
 'Verification requires 34×32 signature bytes, advances each received chain through its remaining 255-digit rounds, hashes the endpoints and compares the resulting public key. This explains how verification can finish a chain without knowing its starting secret.'
], 'The C++ counterpart implements the required hash chains with existing MiniData.sha3 and stores optional private/public state. These detailed steps describe the native body; the Java wrapper delegates to bundled Bouncy Castle classes whose source bodies were not available in this repository search. Compatibility evidence therefore comes from executing the Java library through the oracle, not from claiming that its implementation was read.',
 'The wots branch crosses seed lengths 0, 31, 32, 33 and 64 with message lengths 0, 1, 32 and 65. Successful outputs compare public key and every signature byte, then verify. Derived cases mutate the message/public key and shorten or extend the signature, testing rejection/error behavior. Matching those vectors does not constitute a security proof.',
 'Why does a signature reveal a point partway along each hash chain rather than the private chain start? Explain why the usage counter belongs in TreeKey/wallet handling rather than this wrapper.',
 'Short seeds, default construction and malformed signature sizes have Java null/library failure behavior that the native std::invalid_argument/optional failures do not exactly reproduce. This lesson describes a one-time scheme; repeated signing with the same private key requires explicit application policy.')
